  "state": {
    "case_id": "246",
    "case_summary": "A 44-year-old woman presented with a 4-month history of severe occipital and temporal headaches that were paroxysmal, aggravated by changes in position, and associated with bilateral blurry vision and morning projectile vomiting. Neurological examination showed a kinetic and static cerebellar syndrome with ataxia, imbalance, uncoordinated movements, bilateral nystagmus, and stage I bilateral papilledema. Routine blood tests were unremarkable.\n\nNoncontrast CT of the head showed no abnormalities. Brain MRI revealed an inferior median oval lesion of the cerebellar vermis measuring 40 × 31 × 29 mm, hypointense on T1-weighted images, heterogeneously hyperintense on T2-weighted images, with heterogeneous enhancement after gadolinium administration. The lesion filled the foramen of Magendie, exerted mass effect on the medulla, and showed no surrounding edema on FLAIR. Diffusion-weighted imaging demonstrated increased signal, and GRE T2* sequences showed no hemorrhage or calcification. Magnetic resonance spectroscopy showed an elevated choline peak and reduced N-acetyl aspartate signal.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Liponeurocytoma\nB. Central neurocytoma was considered but deemed unlikely because “Central neurocytomas can be considered as differential\nC. A lipomatous cerebellar tumor was in the differential, yet preoperative imaging lacked fat signals\nD. Medulloblastoma and ependymoma were important considerations since “These tumors must be differentiated from\nE. Classic medulloblastoma was excluded by histologic criteria, as “a high proliferation index will clearly indicate the\nF. The absence of lipid on CT argued against typical lipomatous neoplasms\nG. None\nH. None\n",
